  "query_preview": "(This evening through Sunday)...\nIssued at 247 PM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nToday through Tuesday night...\n\nMostly quiet conditions are expected early in the period due to \nsurface ridging and low-level dry air in place. Current satellite \nand radar imagery depicts extensive mid to high clouds across \nnorthern portions of central Indiana from an approaching upper wave \nwith some light returns. However, most of this is not reaching the \nground due to dry air. Patchy sprinkles or light drizzle will be \npossible at times through early tonight while the impulse aloft \nmoves through. The mid to high clouds has helped keep temperatures \ncooler, in the low-mid 80s, across the north. \n\nUpper ridging building further into the Ohio Valley will likely \nprovide quiet weather conditions through Tuesday. Guidance does \nsuggest a few subtle impulses riding the northern periphery of the \nridge, but these features should track slightly further north \nresulting in less than mid to high clouds than the disturbance \npushing through today. That said, at least some enhancement of mid \nto high clouds appears likely which could limit diurnal heating \nslightly. \n\nExpect southwesterly surface flow and the building upper ridge to \npromote warming temperatures the next few days. Look for highs to \nreach the upper 80s to low 90s by Monday with highs then approaching \nthe low to mid 90s on Tuesday. Heat indices on Tuesday could be near \nthe low 100s. A Heat Advisory may be needed for Tuesday, but will \nhold off for now given some forecast uncertainty.\n\nWednesday through Thursday...\n\nConfidence remains higher than usual that the hot and humid \nconditions will persist through the middle of this week. Long range \nguidance depicts the aforementioned upper ridge retrograding and \nthen amplifying across the central CONUS while troughing over the \nNortheast. Expect highs in the 90s with peak heat indices approaching\nadvisory criteria. Meanwhile, lows in the 70s will provide little\nrelief from the heat and humid. \n\nThere is some uncertainty in the forecast towards Thursday regarding \nmax temperatures as an upper wave may move through the area as \nnorthwest flow begins to develop aloft. Anomalous moisture in place \nby Thursday along with sufficient forcing from the approaching \ndisturbance supports the potential for isolated convection. A few \nstrong storms cannot be ruled out given at least moderate \ndestabilization and some enhancement of mid to upper level flow.\n\nFriday into the weekend... \n\nConfidence in exact details decreases towards the end of this week \ndue to diverging model solutions. Long range guidance generally \ndepicts the upper ridge across the central CONUS continuing to \namplify with persistent northwest flow across central Indiana. \nMultiple shortwaves aloft could move through during this period \nsupporting the potential for at least isolated convection at times. \nIn addition, a cold front may slowly push south late Friday through \nthe weekend. This will provide additional forcing for convection \nwhile also gradually cooling temperatures into Sunday. Greater cloud \ncover and low rain chances should help keep temperatures cooler as \nwell. Look for highs mostly in the 80s to near 90F across the far \nsouth by this weekend.",
